WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
THE STATE OF EUROPE:
Once upon a time, Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:08.000
was the most domineering force in the entire&nbsp;
world. They controlled swathes of land across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.720
the entire globe extracting resources, manpower,&nbsp;
and wealth for their own prosperity. Since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.080
Europe’s outdated and predatory system&nbsp;
of colonialism and imperialism has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:25.720
replaced by capitalism which is ironically also&nbsp;
rather predatory. And for the longest time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:31.520
it seemed that Europe was thriving in this new&nbsp;
world order. They had dozens of heavy weights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:38.280
in the Fortune 500 in the pharmaceutical, banking,&nbsp;
energy, and fashion industries. But, there is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:44.280
industry in which Europe has completely dropped&nbsp;
the ball in: tech. Despite being some of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:51.680
modern and powerful countries in the world, Europe&nbsp;
only has 2 tech companies in the Fortune 100,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:58.160
companies that you probably never even heard&nbsp;
of of: ASML and SAP. And while ASML and SAP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:03.160
are no doubt champions in their own right,&nbsp;
put next to American big tech champions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:09.800
they pale in comparison. ASML currently stands&nbsp;
at a $272 billion market cap while SAP stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:16.040
at a $181 billion market cap. For perspective,&nbsp;
there have been days that Apple has lost SAP’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:21.920
entire market cap in a singular day. And that’s&nbsp;
just comparing the top players. If we go down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.280
list, it looks even worse for Europe. Europe’s&nbsp;
8th largest tech company doesn’t even make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.880
Fortune 500 list, and the only globally relevant&nbsp;
European tech startup that immediately comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.600
mind is Spotify. Now, I can already hear&nbsp;
some of you saying that this is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.680
a good thing. It’s a good thing that Europe’s&nbsp;
economy isn’t dominated by dozens of big tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:49.080
monpolistic corporations. And I definitely get&nbsp;
where you’re coming from, but at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:54.960
this is very much a massive disadvantage for&nbsp;
Europe as they’re starting be viewed as the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:08.055
world when it comes to tech. So, how did Europe&nbsp;
get left so far behind when it comes to tech?

00:02:08.055 --> 00:02:09.960
THE DISPARITY VISUALIZED:
Now, it’s easy to be apathetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.920
to big tech companies. Boo hoo, Europe doesn’t&nbsp;
have any mega tech companies that control every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:20.744
aspect of our lives. But, one of the positives of&nbsp;
big tech is that they’ve created millions of high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.744 --> 00:02:26.360
paying jobs; however, this doesn’t seem to have&nbsp;
really affected Europe nearly as much. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:31.040
when it comes to compensation, European tech&nbsp;
workers are only earning about half as much as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.240
American tech workers for the same jobs. Take&nbsp;
entry level software engineers for example. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:43.040
the top American tech cities, we’re talking&nbsp;
about 128 in San Francisco and 133 in NYC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:49.600
In the top European tech cities, however, we’re&nbsp;
talking about 63 in London and 60 in Amsterdam.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.720
And this disparity goes all the way to the top.&nbsp;
For director of engineering, we’re talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:03.480
202 in San Francisco and 174 in NYC, but for&nbsp;
the same job, it’s only 124 in London and 101&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:09.680
in Amsterdam. And let’s not forget, it’s not&nbsp;
like London is cheap to live in. If anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:17.120
London is a tier 2 city in terms of cost of living&nbsp;
for which tech generally pays 15% less but not 50%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.800
less. You might be saying that that’s because&nbsp;
Europe doesn’t have ultra rich tech companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:27.720
that can pay as much and you’re right. But, almost&nbsp;
every American tech company has a European base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:33.080
and they’re now using Europe as a cheap labor&nbsp;
market. And I’m not even talking about Eastern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:38.280
European countries like Poland and Ukraine. I’m&nbsp;
actually talking about top cities like London.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:45.720
Take Facebook, for example. For level 5 engineers,&nbsp;
the pay average compensation of $421,000. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:51.040
wanna guess how much they pay L5 engineers in&nbsp;
London though? Well, for the most part, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:57.600
pay something in the low $200s. Some London L5&nbsp;
engineers even earn as little as the $130s. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:02.280
I’m not saying that you should feel bad for any&nbsp;
of these people. All of these people are obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:08.040
extremely well off whether they’re making $400&nbsp;
or $200 or $130, but putting aside the specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:13.520
numbers, think about it from a macro scale.&nbsp;
Imagine you’re the United Kingdom. For centuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:18.160
you ruled the entire world. People used to&nbsp;
say that the sun never set on the British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:23.960
empire. You’re not as powerful as you once used&nbsp;
to be but you’re still a world power. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:30.960
you still have the 6th largest GDP in the world&nbsp;
coming in at $3 trillion. But despite all of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:37.240
America has the audacity to come into your country&nbsp;
and pay your citizens half the compensation or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:43.880
less. I don’t know about you but if I was the UK,&nbsp;
I’d feel pretty insulted, but it gets even worse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:49.240
You see, European tech not only compares poorly&nbsp;
to America but it also compares poorly to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:54.360
developing world. Take Google India for example.&nbsp;
Google generally pays slightly less than Facebook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.360 --> 00:05:00.040
like 5% or so. But, you wanna guess how much&nbsp;
L5 Google engineers are making in India. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:06.400
a lot them are earning high $100s. Aka, the&nbsp;
top 25% of Indian FAANG engineers are making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:12.160
more than the bottom 25% of European FAANG&nbsp;
engineers. And that isn't meant as a hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.120
against India. If anything, it’s actually the&nbsp;
exact opposite. All this shows is that India&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:21.600
is very much heading the direction of becoming&nbsp;
a first world country while Europe is heading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:27.840
in the exact opposite direction all because&nbsp;
of tech. And that’s just looking at the UK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:33.680
one of the most prosperous European countries.&nbsp;
So, Europe as whole compares even less favorably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:46.343
to the modern tech industry which brings us to the&nbsp;
question of what in the world happened to Europe?

00:05:46.343 --> 00:05:47.680
EUROPEAN CULTURE:
Researching for this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.320
I was hoping to find some sort pivotal event that&nbsp;
completely changed the trajectories of American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:59.160
tech and European tech but there was no such&nbsp;
singular event. Rather, it seems that the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:05.320
state of European tech is very much a culmination&nbsp;
of decades of evolving European culture. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:11.000
Europeans tend to be more practical. They&nbsp;
actually want to live life and not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:16.840
live to work and this ideology has been worked&nbsp;
into their laws themselves. America is the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:22.000
advanced economy that does not guarantee&nbsp;
paid time off. All EU countries, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:27.160
mandate that employers provide atleast 20 paid&nbsp;
vacation days every year. And many countries like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:32.960
France actually mandate 30 paid vacation days or&nbsp;
6 weeks per year. And all of that is in addition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:38.760
to paid public holidays and usually unlimited&nbsp;
sick days. What this means is that American tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:45.160
workers only get about 2-3 weeks of vacation time&nbsp;
while European tech owrkers usually get 7-8 weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:50.200
of vacation time. But, the amount of vacation&nbsp;
time Americans get doesn’t even matter because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:56.240
Americans just don’t take vacations. Many tech&nbsp;
companies like Netflix actually offer unlimited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.600
paid time off because they’re extremely confident&nbsp;
that their employees won't abuse it. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:08.040
the average American with unlimited PTO only&nbsp;
takes 10 days or 2 weeks off per year. Europeans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:13.880
on the other hand actually tend to use all of&nbsp;
their vacation time. In France for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:20.080
it’s common to take off all of August. And&nbsp;
that’s just vacation time. On a weekly basis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:26.640
Europeans tend to work 35 to 40 hours while&nbsp;
Americans tend to 40 to 45 hours. And if you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:32.240
at the more competitive Americans and places like&nbsp;
India and China, it’s actually more like 70 to 80&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:38.520
hours. Now, studies have shown that the European&nbsp;
approach produces more productivity per hour, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:43.680
in terms of overall productivity, American tech&nbsp;
workers generally come out far ahead of European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:50.040
tech workers simply because they work 500 to a&nbsp;
1000 hours more per year. As such, tech companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:56.080
are unsurprisingly willing to also pay American&nbsp;
engineers a lot more than European engineers. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:02.000
actual productivity only tells half the story. The&nbsp;
other half is due to vastly different compensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:07.640
laws and policies especially when it comes to&nbsp;
benefits. In America, on top of your cash salary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:13.840
you can usually expect a bonus, up to a 3% match&nbsp;
on a 401K plan, and discounted health insurance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:19.040
In Europe, however, you can usually expect a&nbsp;
bonus, up to a 10% contribution to a pension fund,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:23.400
and fully paid for private health insurance.&nbsp;
In general, Europeans tend to get far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:28.800
small benefits here and there and that’s just&nbsp;
what’s apparant on the compensation package.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:33.560
Behind the scenes, European employers have to&nbsp;
pay far more in taxes. Take social security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:40.120
tax for example. American employers only have&nbsp;
to 7.65% in social security tax. In the UK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:47.080
employers have to pay 13.8%. In Germany, employers&nbsp;
have to pay 20%. And in France, employers have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:53.280
pay a whopping 45%. Obviously, companies are going&nbsp;
to take all of this into account when making job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:59.640
offers to Europeans leading to far less upfront&nbsp;
compensation. So, all of that kind of explains the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:05.160
quantitative side of lower European compensation,&nbsp;
but the reality is that the biggest factor leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:13.220
to Europe being left behind actually has&nbsp;
nothing to do with objective reasoning.

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:18.960
CONTENTMENT:
It’s easy to write off the European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:23.200
tech industry using some sort of quantitative&nbsp;
measure whether it’s European’s working less or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:28.200
higher taxes or more benefits or whatever the&nbsp;
case may be. But, the reality is that there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:34.840
only one reason why the disparity between American&nbsp;
tech and European tech is so large. It’s because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:41.560
Europe has something that America never had and&nbsp;
likely never will: contentment. Whether its on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:46.600
macro scale where big tech companies are trying&nbsp;
to grow to billions of users or on a micro scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:51.640
where employees are vying for the next promotion&nbsp;
or bonus, Europeans often just don’t care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:56.600
atleast not nearly as much as Americans. Take&nbsp;
employee tenure for example. The median employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.600 --> 00:10:03.320
tenure in America is 4.8 years while the median&nbsp;
employee tenure in Europe is over double at 9.8&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:08.880
years. This is primarily because Europeans are far&nbsp;
less likely to constantly jump around companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:13.600
looking for the best compensation and title&nbsp;
possible. And this isn’t to say that Europeans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:19.560
aim low. They absolutely do want a solid income,&nbsp;
healthy benefits, and a comfortable retirement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:24.840
But that’s also all they want. They’re not looking&nbsp;
for the mega millions that American tech workers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:30.680
are after, and likely the best example of this&nbsp;
is stock based compensation. You know how we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:36.000
talking about European tech workers only being&nbsp;
paid half of American tech workers? Well, that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:41.200
only if you look at total compensation. If you’re&nbsp;
just comparing cash salary and bonus, European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:46.360
tech compensation is actually quite comparable&nbsp;
to American tech compensation espeically when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:50.880
factor in more paid time off, larger retirement&nbsp;
contributions, and better health insurance. Where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:57.640
European compensation actually falls way behind&nbsp;
is stock based compensation. Let’s go back to our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:04.960
American L5 Facebook engineer for example. Out&nbsp;
of their total comp of $421,000, a $183,000 or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:10.720
nearly half is just stock. In London, however,&nbsp;
these same engineers are often paid as little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:17.120
as $20 to $30k in stock, or only about 15% as much&nbsp;
as their American counterparts. This isn’t because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:22.200
big tech is snubbing these employees but largely&nbsp;
because Europeans tend to value stock compensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:27.160
far less than Americans. In America, especially&nbsp;
with startups, engineers are often willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:33.120
forgo their entire cash compensation or take a&nbsp;
very modest salary in exchange for more equity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:39.400
In Europe, however, it’s the exact opposite. Most&nbsp;
would much rather have a consistent and reliable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:45.160
cash income instead of equity. And when you take&nbsp;
the most lucrative part of tech compensation out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:50.160
of the equation, well, European compensation&nbsp;
is what you’re left with and the higher you go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:56.360
in title, the bigger the disparity. European L7&nbsp;
Facebook engineers for example are earning up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.360 --> 00:12:02.520
half a million dollars less. And honestly, I don’t&nbsp;
see this changing as this isn’t just the case with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:08.920
tech but basically every industry. US doctors&nbsp;
average over 300 while many European doctors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:15.880
don’t even cross 100. US lawyers can ofteen cross&nbsp;
200 while European lawyers often don’t cross 100.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:21.200
It’s this same trend is carrying over to the tech&nbsp;
industry as well just at a different level. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:25.600
given that the Europeans that do want to earn&nbsp;
more money and start big companies just come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:32.400
America, it’s likely that this disparity will&nbsp;
only grow with time. But hey, maybe that’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:38.360
such a bad thing. Europeans may not be home to&nbsp;
the highest salaries or the biggest companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:44.000
but they are home to the happiest people on&nbsp;
the planet, and in the end, isn’t that what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:49.200
really matters? With historically high interest&nbsp;
rates, the appeal bills and bonds is the higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

